# Normalize line endings for text files to LF, both in the repository and in
# every working tree that checks it out — including a Windows CI runner or
# contributor machine where git's own installer default is
# `core.autocrlf=true`.
#
# Without this, a file git considers text (source, config, packaging scripts)
# round-trips through Windows checkout as CRLF even though it was authored and
# committed as LF. That silently breaks anything that reads the file's exact
# bytes: a golden fixture compared byte-for-byte, a script that searches a
# file for an LF-terminated line, a shell script run under a strict shell.
#
# `text=auto` defers to git's own text/binary heuristic per path, so this
# cannot corrupt a binary asset (image, font, archive, compiled binary) that
# happens to live anywhere in the tree — only paths git already classifies as
# text are affected. `eol=lf` pins the working-tree representation to LF on
# every platform, so the same bytes are read back regardless of which OS
# checked the repository out.
* text=auto eol=lf
